  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|{x}^{3}-5|}{2-{x}^{3}}?$",
  "worked_solution": "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|{x}^{3}-5|=({x}^{3}-5).$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|{x}^{3}-5|}{2-{x}^{3}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-5}{2-{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-5}{2-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(1-\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$",
